# Copy this file to /etc/abuseipdb-proxy/abuseipdb-proxy.env and fill in
# your real values. install.sh does this for you automatically and asks
# for the API key interactively — this file is mainly here as a reference
# for manual setups or for anyone reading the repo before installing.
#
# IMPORTANT: never commit the real abuseipdb-proxy.env file. It's already
# covered by .gitignore, but double-check before pushing if you copy it
# somewhere else.

# Required. Get yours at https://www.abuseipdb.com/account/api
ABUSEIPDB_API_KEY=your_api_key_here

# Any secret-like value in this file (the API key above, the fallback
# key, the CrowdSec bouncer key, the shared secret, notification
# tokens/webhook URLs) can instead be provided via a file, using the
# Docker/Podman secrets convention: set "{VAR}_FILE" to a file path
# instead of the value itself. Useful for pulling from Vaultwarden/a
# secrets manager via a mount rather than having the plain value sit in
# this file. If both are set, the _FILE variant wins. Example:
# ABUSEIPDB_API_KEY_FILE=/run/secrets/abuseipdb_api_key

# Optional, shown here with their defaults.
# ABUSEIPDB_PROXY_PORT=9999
# Only change this from loopback-only in an isolated environment (e.g.
# inside Docker's own network isolation) — never expose it directly to
# an untrusted network.
# ABUSEIPDB_LISTEN_ADDRESS=127.0.0.1
# ABUSEIPDB_CACHE_FILE=/var/lib/abuseipdb-proxy/cache.db
# SQLite is the only cache backend (ABUSEIPDB_CACHE_BACKEND=json was
# removed entirely in 3.0.0 — see CHANGELOG.md). If you're upgrading
# from a v1.x/v2.x install with an existing cache.json sitting right next
# to where cache.db will be created, it's imported automatically the
# first time this runs — no action needed, the old file is kept as
# cache.json.migrated. If your old cache.json lives somewhere else
# (a custom ABUSEIPDB_CACHE_FILE path), migrate it explicitly first:
#   abuseipdb_proxy.py --migrate-to-sqlite /path/to/old/cache.json
# SQLite storage tuning — defaults are already SSD-friendly (WAL avoids
# rewriting whole pages on small writes; NORMAL sync skips the second
# fsync-per-transaction WAL asks for). Rarely worth changing; FULL trades
# some of that write-amplification benefit for extra durability if you're
# especially worried about a crash mid-write on flaky storage (e.g. an
# SD card):
# ABUSEIPDB_SQLITE_JOURNAL_MODE=WAL
# ABUSEIPDB_SQLITE_SYNCHRONOUS=NORMAL
# ABUSEIPDB_REPORT_WINDOW=905
# ABUSEIPDB_REPORT_WINDOW_LOW=905
# ABUSEIPDB_REPORT_WINDOW_MEDIUM=905
# ABUSEIPDB_REPORT_WINDOW_HIGH=905
# Optional: override the window for specific AbuseIPDB category numbers,
# finer-grained than the severity tiers above. Comma-separated
# category=seconds pairs, e.g. give SQL Injection (16) and Exploited Host
# (20) a longer window than the rest of severity 3:
# ABUSEIPDB_REPORT_WINDOW_CATEGORIES=16=1800,20=3600
# ABUSEIPDB_MAX_RETRIES=3
# ABUSEIPDB_RETRY_DELAY=900
# ABUSEIPDB_DRY_RUN=false

# Optional extra layers on top of ABUSEIPDB_LISTEN_ADDRESS (see above) —
# useful mainly when the listener isn't loopback-only. Both off by
# default. Allowlist accepts comma-separated IPs/CIDRs; the shared secret
# is checked via an X-Proxy-Secret header, which you'd add to the
# "headers:" block in abuseipdb.yaml:
# ABUSEIPDB_ALLOWED_SOURCE_IPS=
# ABUSEIPDB_SHARED_SECRET=

# Safety net, not a normal throttle: caps how many requests the proxy
# handles at once (each gets its own thread since v2.8.0). 0 disables the
# limit entirely. Rejects with a 503 immediately rather than queuing —
# meant to catch a genuine misconfiguration/bug (a scenario loop, a flood
# of decisions), not to smooth over ordinary bursts:
# ABUSEIPDB_MAX_CONCURRENT_REQUESTS=50

# Reserve part of the daily AbuseIPDB quota for higher-severity findings,
# so a burst of low-severity noise can't use it all up before a real
# exploit attempt shows up later the same day. Both 0 (disabled) by
# default; only takes effect once the proxy has seen a remaining-quota
# count from AbuseIPDB. MEDIUM should normally be >= HIGH, since it also
# covers severity 2:
# ABUSEIPDB_QUOTA_RESERVE_MEDIUM=0
# ABUSEIPDB_QUOTA_RESERVE_HIGH=0

# A report held back by the quota reserve above is not dropped — it's
# rescheduled to re-check the quota after this many seconds, in case it's
# freed up again later. Default 300 (5 minutes):
# ABUSEIPDB_QUOTA_RESERVE_RECHECK_DELAY=300

# Skip reporting IPs AbuseIPDB itself already marks as whitelisted (e.g.
# well-known crawlers/CDNs that opted in). Off by default: this calls
# AbuseIPDB's /v2/check endpoint synchronously on the request path, which
# consumes its own separate daily quota from /v2/report and adds network
# latency per cache miss (mitigated by the TTL cache below):
# ABUSEIPDB_SKIP_WHITELISTED=false
# ABUSEIPDB_WHITELIST_CACHE_TTL=86400

# Redact matches of these semicolon-separated regexes from the comment
# text before it's sent to AbuseIPDB (whose comments are public) —
# useful if your CrowdSec comment templates might ever echo something
# internal (a hostname, a path). Off by default:
# ABUSEIPDB_COMMENT_SCRUB_PATTERNS=
# ABUSEIPDB_COMMENT_SCRUB_REPLACEMENT=[redacted]

# A second AbuseIPDB API key (a separate account) to switch to once the
# primary key's daily report quota is exhausted (detected via an HTTP 429
# on /v2/report), instead of just queuing everything for retry until
# midnight UTC. Switches back to the primary automatically the first time
# a new UTC day is seen. Off by default:
# ABUSEIPDB_API_KEY_FALLBACK=

# CrowdSec decision reconciliation (run via 'abuseipdb_proxy.py --reconcile',
# e.g. from an hourly timer — see abuseipdb-proxy-reconcile.service/.timer).
# Compares CrowdSec's currently active decisions against this proxy's own
# report cache and reports anything missing — catches reports lost to
# proxy downtime. Requires a bouncer API key ('cscli bouncers add <name>'
# on the CrowdSec host); does nothing without one. Categories/severity
# come from the decision's own scenario name (same mapping abuseipdb.yaml
# uses); RECONCILE_SEVERITY/_CATEGORIES below are only a fallback for
# decisions with no scenario name at all (e.g. added manually):
# ABUSEIPDB_CROWDSEC_LAPI_URL=http://127.0.0.1:8080
# ABUSEIPDB_CROWDSEC_BOUNCER_KEY=
# ABUSEIPDB_RECONCILE_SEVERITY=2
# ABUSEIPDB_RECONCILE_CATEGORIES=15

# Private/reserved IP filtering (on by default). Extra ranges/IPs to
# exclude beyond the built-in RFC1918/loopback/link-local/CGNAT list,
# comma-separated (single IPs or CIDR):
# ABUSEIPDB_IGNORE_PRIVATE=true
# ABUSEIPDB_IGNORE_IPS=

# Log volume control. Off by default to stay quiet under high-volume
# setups (e.g. a honeypot under active attack) — a periodic summary line
# is logged instead. See "Log volume" in the README for the full picture,
# including a systemd-level rate limit safety net in the .service file.
# ABUSEIPDB_VERBOSE_LOGGING=false
# ABUSEIPDB_SUMMARY_INTERVAL=300
# "text" (traditional single-line format) or "json" (one JSON object per
# line, for Loki/ELK/Graylog etc.):
# ABUSEIPDB_LOG_FORMAT=text

# How often (seconds) this proxy re-checks its persisted pending/retry
# tables for rows with no matching in-memory timer in this process, and
# re-arms them. Mainly relevant if you use --reconcile: a report it
# triggers that needs a retry only has a timer in that short-lived CLI
# process, which is gone the instant it exits — this periodic check is
# what actually picks that retry back up in the long-running service.
# 0 disables it (matches pre-3.0.3 behavior: a retry/pending row from
# another process is only ever picked up by resume_state_from_cache() at
# this service's own next restart). Default 60:
# ABUSEIPDB_ORPHAN_RESCAN_INTERVAL=60

# Warns (once per continuous episode, via your configured notification
# backend(s) below) if the combined pending+retry backlog stays at or
# above this size continuously for at least
# ABUSEIPDB_RETRY_BACKLOG_WARN_AGE seconds. A count check alone would be
# noisy -- dozens of pending escalations at once is normal on a busy
# install -- so this only fires once that count has stayed elevated
# rather than draining back down the way normal traffic does, which
# usually means AbuseIPDB's API itself is down or erroring rather than
# a problem with any single IP. 0 disables. Defaults: size 20, age 900s:
# ABUSEIPDB_RETRY_BACKLOG_WARN_SIZE=20
# ABUSEIPDB_RETRY_BACKLOG_WARN_AGE=900

# One-time-per-day notification when the AbuseIPDB daily quota (tracked
# from the API's own rate-limit headers) drops to or below this many
# reports remaining. The notification also includes a projected
# exhaustion time once enough of today's consumption rate is known:
# ABUSEIPDB_QUOTA_WARN_THRESHOLD=50

# Endpoints are OFF by default — opt in explicitly (both are already
# bound to 127.0.0.1 only either way):
# ABUSEIPDB_ENABLE_HEALTH=false
# ABUSEIPDB_ENABLE_METRICS=false

# Send a low-priority notification (via any configured backend above)
# every time the proxy starts. Off by default.
# ABUSEIPDB_NOTIFY_ON_START=false

# Optional alerting — configure any combination of these. Each backend
# activates itself as soon as its URL (and token, where required) is set.
# Test with: python3 abuseipdb_proxy.py --test-notify
# ABUSEIPDB_NOTIFY_NAME=CrowdSec Smart AbuseIPDB Proxy
# ABUSEIPDB_GOTIFY_URL=https://gotify.example.com
# ABUSEIPDB_GOTIFY_TOKEN=
# ABUSEIPDB_NTFY_URL=https://ntfy.example.com/my-topic
# ABUSEIPDB_NTFY_TOKEN=
# ABUSEIPDB_WEBHOOK_URL=
# ABUSEIPDB_SLACK_WEBHOOK_URL=https://hooks.slack.com/services/...
# ABUSEIPDB_DISCORD_WEBHOOK_URL=https://discord.com/api/webhooks/...
# Matrix needs all three of these (no webhook, posts via the Client-Server
# API as a dedicated bot user you've already invited into the room):
# ABUSEIPDB_MATRIX_HOMESERVER_URL=https://matrix.org
# ABUSEIPDB_MATRIX_ACCESS_TOKEN=
# ABUSEIPDB_MATRIX_ROOM_ID=!roomid:matrix.org
# Telegram needs both:
# ABUSEIPDB_TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN=
# ABUSEIPDB_TELEGRAM_CHAT_ID=
# Home Assistant needs both (calls notify.<service> over HA's REST API
# using a Long-Lived Access Token from a HA user profile — no separate
# bridge needed). NOTIFY_SERVICE defaults to "notify" (the generic
# notify.notify service); set it to e.g. "mobile_app_myphone" to target
# one device instead:
# ABUSEIPDB_HOMEASSISTANT_URL=https://homeassistant.local:8123
# ABUSEIPDB_HOMEASSISTANT_TOKEN=
# ABUSEIPDB_HOMEASSISTANT_NOTIFY_SERVICE=notify

# How many timestamped snapshots --backup keeps before pruning the oldest
# (see "CLI reference" in the README):
# ABUSEIPDB_BACKUP_RETENTION=14
